From 89580dec2e9dc4928cab9cee066f43b4e6071945 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: local <local@localhost>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 13:25:05 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 10/19] Generator: occupancy-aware draft length
 (EXL3_DRAFT_ROW_BUDGET)

A batched verify forward is (active sequences) x (draft + 1) rows. Past the
multi-row GEMV's row limit every linear drops to a much slower GEMM, so a
fixed draft length that is right for one job is wrong for four. With
EXL3_DRAFT_ROW_BUDGET=N, each round's draft length becomes
min(num_draft_tokens, N // rows - 1), at least 1, in every draft mode
(DFlash2, AR draft model, MTP, n-gram). For example, N = 16 with 7 draft
tokens gives 7 at 1-2 jobs and 3 at 4 jobs; N = 32 keeps 7 up to 4 jobs. The
floor of one draft token keeps the AR and MTP draft caches advancing, since
only drafting advances them. Unset or 0 leaves the draft length unchanged.

When the budget shortens a DFlash2 round, the drafter computes only what
the round uses: the target head and the selector walk run over block rows
1..window instead of all 8 rows. The anchor row's logits were always
discarded, and later rows only cost lm_head rows. The walk is sequential
and the head row-wise, so the first window proposals are unchanged. They
are bit-identical whenever the head still runs the WMMA GEMV (2+ rows):
it gives a row the same value at any row tile. A single-row head call
takes the fdot2 GEMV instead and may differ by rounding. The
block forward itself stays at the native 8 rows, since block attention is
bidirectional.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
